---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import WeatherSim from '../../simulators/weather/WeatherSim.astro';
import { weatherQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/weather';

const toc = [
  { id: 'atmosphere', label: 'The atmosphere' },
  { id: 'ingredients', label: 'The ingredients of weather' },
  { id: 'clouds', label: 'How clouds form' },
  { id: 'stability', label: 'Stable and unstable air' },
  { id: 'pressure', label: 'High and low pressure' },
  { id: 'precipitation', label: 'Rain, snow and storms' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'The numbers behind the model' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ahrens, C. D., & Henson, R. (2021). Meteorology Today (13th ed.). Cengage.' },
  { text: 'Lawrence, M. G. (2005). The relationship between relative humidity and the dewpoint temperature in moist air. Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society, 86(2), 225–233.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1175/BAMS-86-2-225' },
  { text: 'Met Office. Clouds: how clouds form and the different types of cloud.', url: 'https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/types-of-weather/clouds' },
  { text: 'NOAA National Weather Service. JetStream: An Online School for Weather.', url: 'https://www.weather.gov/jetstream/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="earth-science"
  topic="weather"
  description="Interactive weather simulator: set temperature, humidity and pressure, watch rising air cool to its dew point, and see when you get clear skies, cumulus clouds, thunderstorms, snow or fog."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={weatherQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Why are some days cloudless while others build towering thunderclouds? It comes down to warm air rising, cooling
      and reaching its dew point. Set the temperature, humidity and pressure, release a bubble of warm air from the
      ground, and watch what the sky does.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <WeatherSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Try the example days, or set the <strong>ground temperature</strong>, <strong>humidity</strong> and <strong>pressure system</strong> yourself.</li>
      <li>Watch the <strong>rising air</strong> (orange) cool as it climbs. When it reaches its dew point it turns into cloud.</li>
      <li>On the chart, the rising air keeps going while it is <strong>warmer than the surrounding air</strong> (orange line right of the blue line).</li>
      <li>Read the cloud type and precipitation. Make the air more humid and watch the cloud base drop.</li>
      <li>Find the settings that make a thunderstorm, snow showers and fog.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="atmosphere">The atmosphere</h2>
  <p>
    Weather happens in the <strong>troposphere</strong>, the lowest layer of the atmosphere, about 8–16 km deep. It
    contains most of the air’s mass and almost all its water vapour. On average the temperature falls by about 6.5 °C for
    every kilometre you climb, which is why mountain tops are cold. At the top of the troposphere, the
    <strong>tropopause</strong>, the temperature stops falling, acting as a lid on most clouds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ingredients">The ingredients of weather</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> how warm the air is, set mainly by sunshine and where the air came from.</li>
    <li><strong>Humidity:</strong> how much water vapour the air holds. <strong>Relative humidity</strong> compares this with the maximum the air could hold at that temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Air pressure:</strong> the weight of the air above, about 1,013 hPa at sea level on average.</li>
    <li><strong>Wind:</strong> air moving from high pressure towards low pressure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="clouds">How clouds form</h2>
  <p>
    When the Sun warms the ground, the air above it warms, becomes less dense and rises. As it rises the pressure around it
    falls, so it <strong>expands and cools</strong>: about 9.8 °C per kilometre while it is unsaturated. Eventually it cools
    to its <strong>dew point</strong>, the temperature at which its water vapour condenses onto tiny particles of dust and
    salt, forming billions of cloud droplets. That height is the flat <strong>cloud base</strong> you often see under
    cumulus clouds. Air can also be forced up over mountains or along weather fronts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stability">Stable and unstable air</h2>
  <p>
    Whether a cloud stays small or grows into a giant depends on the surrounding air. If the rising air stays
    <strong>warmer than its surroundings</strong>, it keeps rising, like a hot-air balloon: the atmosphere is
    <strong>unstable</strong>. Inside a cloud, condensation releases <strong>latent heat</strong>, so cloudy air cools only
    about 4–7 °C per kilometre and can keep rising for many kilometres. If the surroundings are warm aloft, the air is
    <strong>stable</strong>: rising air soon becomes cooler than its surroundings and sinks back, so clouds stay flat or do
    not form.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pressure">High and low pressure</h2>
  <p>
    In a <strong>high-pressure system</strong> (anticyclone), air slowly sinks and warms. This creates a warm layer, an
    <strong>inversion</strong>, that caps rising air, so highs bring settled, often sunny weather, and calm nights with fog
    or frost. In a <strong>low-pressure system</strong> (depression), air converges and rises, so clouds build easily and the
    weather is unsettled, wet and windy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="precipitation">Rain, snow and storms</h2>
  <p>
    Cloud droplets are far too small to fall. In deep clouds, droplets collide and merge, and ice crystals grow at the
    expense of droplets, until they are heavy enough to fall as <strong>precipitation</strong>. If the air below the cloud
    is below about 2 °C, it reaches the ground as <strong>snow</strong>. In the tallest clouds, cumulonimbus, strong
    updrafts separate electric charges, producing <strong>lightning</strong> and thunder, and can carry ice up and down
    until it grows into <strong>hail</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">The numbers behind the model</h2>
  <p><code class="formula">Td = c·γ / (b − γ), γ = ln(RH/100) + b·T/(c + T) · cloud base ≈ 125 m × (T − Td)</code></p>
  <p>
    The simulator uses the Magnus formula for the dew point (b = 17.62, c = 243.12 °C), the dry rate of 9.8 °C per km
    below the cloud, and the moist adiabatic rate inside it, which depends on temperature and pressure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Relative humidity</dt>
    <dd>Water vapour in the air as a percentage of the maximum it could hold at that temperature.</dd>
    <dt>Condensation</dt>
    <dd>Water vapour turning into liquid water droplets.</dd>
    <dt>Lapse rate</dt>
    <dd>How fast temperature falls with height.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Clouds are made of water vapour.”</strong> Vapour is invisible; clouds are tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals.</li>
    <li><strong>“Higher up is warmer because it’s closer to the Sun.”</strong> The air is heated mainly by the ground, so it gets colder with height.</li>
    <li><strong>“100 % humidity means it is raining.”</strong> It means the air is saturated; you might have fog or cloud without rain.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rising air expands and cools; clouds form when it reaches its dew point.</li>
    <li>More humid air means a lower cloud base.</li>
    <li>Unstable air (often low pressure) builds tall clouds and storms; stable air (high pressure) keeps skies clear.</li>
    <li>Latent heat released by condensation powers thunderstorms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: a single bubble of air rising through a simplified atmosphere. Real forecasts use measured temperature and humidity at every height, wind, fronts and much more.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
